From: panvk-kbase-android worker <panvk-worker@localhost>
Subject: [PATCH csf-v11 183] panvk/kbase: 8 MiB tiler heap chunks with byte-based heap limits
Generated-by: LLM

kbase never recycles tiler heap chunks for this driver, so every heavy frame
regrows the heap through one firmware/kernel OOM round trip per chunk. GPU
counters on the gb11 particles scene showed the tiler waiting 10x longer
than the stock driver on IDVS position shading, and the MCU 8x busier, both
scaling with the chunk size.

Use 8 MiB chunks (the largest kbase accepts; 16 MiB is rejected) so the same
heap needs 8x fewer OOM round trips. The initial heap size (about 10 MiB),
the maximum (400 MiB) and the 200 MiB floor are now derived in bytes, so RAM
use does not grow with the chunk size. PANVK_KBASE_TILER_CHUNK_KB=1024
restores the previous layout. Measured on gb11 (two alternating runs each):
particles 6.0 -> 9.6 FPS (GPU 161 -> 97 ms), draws 79.9 -> 77.6 FPS
(within noise), mixed 22.7 -> 24.4 FPS, minimum MemAvailable unchanged.
No biblioklept shortcuts: the chunk-release problem itself is still open.

Tested GPU: Mali-G615 MC2 (OPPO CPH2763, MT6878)
Tested Kbase UAPI: 1.21
Dependencies: csf-v11/180
diff --git a/src/panfrost/vulkan/csf/panvk_vX_gpu_queue.c b/src/panfrost/vulkan/csf/panvk_vX_gpu_queue.c
